# .cursorrules — QA / Test Automation (Playwright + Cypress)
# Legacy single-file format. For modern Cursor, use the split rules in cursor/rules/*.mdc instead.
# Place this file at your project root.

You are assisting on an end-to-end test automation codebase. Write tests that are
stable, maintainable, and CI-ready. Follow these rules without exception.

## Universal rules
- One behavior + one user-observable assertion per test.
- Selectors live in page objects / custom commands, never inline in specs.
- Specs read like user stories: call intent methods (login, addToCart), not raw locators.
- Never use hard/arbitrary waits (no waitForTimeout / cy.wait(ms)). Use auto-retrying
  assertions and network interception instead.
- Prefer user-facing locators: role > label/placeholder > text > test-id. Avoid
  nth-child, deep CSS, and XPath.
- Each test provisions and cleans up its own data. No order-dependent or shared mutable state.
- Read base URL and credentials from environment/config. Never hardcode or commit secrets.
- TypeScript strict; no `any`. Async/await throughout.
- Tag critical revenue/auth paths with @smoke.

## Playwright specifics
- Use custom fixtures (test.extend) to inject page objects and a one-line `loggedIn` state.
- Use web-first assertions: await expect(locator).toBeVisible() / toHaveText().
- Config is env-driven: retries on CI only; trace on-first-retry; screenshot/video on failure only.

## Cypress specifics
- Programmatic login via cy.request inside a cy.login() custom command.
- Assert network with cy.intercept + cy.wait('@alias'); never sleep.
- Select via [data-cy=...] attributes.

## Always reject
- waitForTimeout / cy.wait(milliseconds)
- selectors like `div > div:nth-child(3) span`
- logging in through the UI inside every test
- assertions hidden inside action methods
- hardcoded URLs or credentials
- committed test.only / it.only
